<article class="post-body">

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §01 ══ -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Why Coding Agents Fail at Long-Horizon Research</h2>
<p>
  Give an LLM agent 48 hours and an optimizer to improve — it will run many experiments. But each one starts from scratch.
  The agent that just discovered "axis statistics alone aren't enough" doesn't <em>know</em> that when it plans its next trial.
  What it tried, what it learned, and what that implies for future directions — all of it evaporates between runs.
</p>
<p>
  This is the core failure mode of single-trajectory agents on long-horizon research tasks: they are <strong>amnesiac improvers</strong>.
  They can fix a single bug, but they can't build a research strategy. Their token budget is spent re-discovering
  the same dead ends rather than accumulating toward a genuine research insight.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">research trajectory comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to compare</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding-bottom:.5rem">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
      <button class="traj-btn active" id="btn-linear" onclick="setMode('linear')">Without Tree (Linear)</button>
      <button class="traj-btn" id="btn-tree" onclick="setMode('tree')">With Arbor Tree</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="traj-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;border-radius:8px"></canvas>
    <div id="traj-legend" style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#e06c75;display:inline-block"></span>failed — not reused</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#f59e0b;display:inline-block"></span>partial improvement</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#10b981;display:inline-block"></span>merged — verified</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The blue arrows in tree mode show insight propagation — when a leaf experiment fails, its lesson flows upward
  and constrains the hypotheses generated for sibling and future nodes. In linear mode, there are no arrows:
  experiment 4 knows nothing of what experiment 1 learned.
</p>

<div class="callout purple">
  <p><strong>Autonomous Optimization (AO)</strong> — the paper's formalization: given an initial artifact M₀,
  improve it through experimental feedback without step-level human supervision. The key challenge is that
  research feedback is delayed, experiments are expensive, and failed attempts contain information that should guide later search.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §02 ══ -->
<section id="s-tree" data-code="tree">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Hypothesis Tree</div>
<h2>A Persistent Research State</h2>
<p>
  Arbor's solution is a persistent <strong>Hypothesis Tree</strong> that serves as the global research state.
  Every experiment is a node in this tree, and each node binds three things together:
</p>

<div class="node-diagram">
  <div class="node-field h">
    <div class="node-field-label">h — Hypothesis</div>
    <div class="node-field-body">A verifiable or falsifiable claim about how to change the artifact.
    Near-root nodes name broad directions; leaf nodes are concrete interventions an executor can implement.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="node-field i">
    <div class="node-field-label">ι — Insight</div>
    <div class="node-field-body">The reusable interpretation of evidence. Not a raw execution log—a compact semantic memory.
    "Axis statistics alone are not enough. Newton-Schulz geometry is load-bearing."</div>
  </div>
  <div class="node-field m">
    <div class="node-field-label">μ — Metadata</div>
    <div class="node-field-body">Status (pending/running/merged/pruned), dev score, factual result record, and a git branch reference
    to the artifact version the hypothesis produced.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The tree plays three roles simultaneously: a <em>search frontier</em> (which directions are active or pruned),
  a <em>long-term memory</em> (reusable lessons from both successes and failures),
  and an <em>audit trail</em> (every artifact change is linked to the hypothesis that motivated it).
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">math-reasoning data synthesis — actual hypothesis tree from paper</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a node to expand</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div id="tree-svg-container" style="overflow-x:auto">
      <svg id="tree-svg" viewBox="0 0 780 340" style="width:100%;min-width:580px;font-family:var(--ff-mono)"></svg>
    </div>
    <div id="tree-detail" style="margin-top:.75rem;padding:.75rem 1rem;background:var(--card2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;font-size:.75rem;display:none">
      <div id="tree-detail-title" style="font-weight:700;color:var(--text2);margin-bottom:.3rem"></div>
      <div id="tree-detail-body" style="color:var(--muted2);line-height:1.6"></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The tree grows top-down but learns bottom-up. The root node represents the original research goal.
  Each cycle, the coordinator expands promising leaves into children. When a leaf returns evidence,
  its insight propagates upward — nodes close to the root accumulate high-level research understanding
  from many experiments below them.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §03 ══ -->
<section id="s-htr" data-code="htr">
<div class="section-no">03 — HTR Algorithm</div>
<h2>The Six-Step Coordinator Loop</h2>
<p>
  A long-lived coordinator owns the tree and runs six structured steps each cycle. Short-lived executors
  implement individual hypotheses in isolated git worktrees and return compact evidence back to the tree.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">hypothesis tree refinement — coordinator cycle</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">use Prev / Next</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="htr-steps">
      <div class="step-box active">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Observe</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Re-read the tree state: frontier nodes, recent evidence, ancestor insights, and the current best artifact.
          This makes the tree the authoritative state — prevents the coordinator from relying on a lossy conversational history.</div>
          <div class="step-code">V ← Observe(Tree, Mbest)  // shape, root insight, pruned/validated lessons</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Ideate</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Choose a parent and attach k pending child hypotheses to it.
          Ideation is conditioned on tree evidence: validated insights provide assumptions to build on;
          pruned nodes provide negative constraints. Not free-form brainstorming — evidence-grounded refinement.</div>
          <div class="step-code">p ← choose parent; attach k children ← Ideate(p, V)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Select</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Choose which pending leaves to execute next.
          Selection balances expected utility against accumulated evidence from ancestors and siblings.
          A direction may be selected because its failure would clarify an important assumption, not just because its score looks promising.</div>
          <div class="step-code">L ← pending leaves under Select(V)  // frontier control</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Dispatch</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Send selected leaves to independent executor agents, each in an isolated worktree.
          Executors run in parallel — sibling hypotheses yield comparative evidence within one research direction.
          Each executor receives: hypothesis h, ancestor insights, and the current best artifact.</div>
          <div class="step-code">&#123;(sn, rn, ιn, bn)&#125;n∈L ← parallel Executor(hn, ιanc(n), Mbest)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Backpropagate</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Write evidence into leaf nodes, then propagate insights upward through the tree.
          A leaf-level observation ("data-interface mismatch") becomes a direction-level constraint,
          and eventually a global prior that shapes future ideation across all branches.</div>
          <div class="step-code">ιa ← Abstract(&#123;ιc&#125;c∈ch(a))  // upward through path to root</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box">
        <div class="step-num">6</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Decide</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Merge, prune, continue, or pend.
          Promotion is guarded by the held-out merge gate: the candidate is evaluated on Etest in a fresh worktree
          and merged into Mbest only if it improves over current best. Separates exploratory success from verified progress.</div>
          <div class="step-code">if O(Etest(bn†)) &gt; O(Etest(Mbest)) then Mbest ← merge(bn†)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 6</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Crucially, the coordinator never implements anything — that's the executor's job. The coordinator
  sets research strategy; executors do isolated engineering. Low-level execution traces never pollute
  the global research state.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §04 ══ -->
<section id="s-propagation" data-code="propagation">
<div class="section-no">04 — Insight Propagation</div>
<h2>How Local Results Become Global Strategy</h2>
<p>
  The most distinctive feature of HTR isn't the tree structure — it's that insights <em>flow upward</em>.
  When executor results return, the coordinator doesn't just record a score. It extracts a compact semantic
  interpretation and propagates that interpretation up the path to the root.
</p>
<p>
  A leaf-level discovery like <em>"axis statistics alone aren't enough — NS geometry is load-bearing"</em>
  doesn't stay in that one leaf. It gets abstracted into its parent node ("preserve optimizer geometry"),
  then into the root ("maintain full-matrix gradient structure"). Later hypotheses in entirely different
  subtrees then know to avoid replacing Newton-Schulz.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">insight propagation — click experiment to trigger</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">watch the insight flow up</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body" style="padding-bottom:.5rem">
    <canvas id="prop-canvas" style="width:100%;height:340px;display:block;border-radius:8px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-top:.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap">
      <button class="step-nav" style="display:inline-flex;gap:.5rem">
        <button id="prop-run" onclick="runPropagation(false)" style="padding:.3rem .8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">▶ Run Experiment (fail)</button>
        <button id="prop-run-ok" onclick="runPropagation(true)" style="padding:.3rem .8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">▶ Run Experiment (pass)</button>
        <button id="prop-reset" onclick="resetPropagation()" style="padding:.3rem .8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted2)">↺ Reset</button>
      </button>
    </div>
    <div id="prop-status" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;min-height:1.4em"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Compare this to the <em>w/o insight feedback</em> ablation in the paper: removing upward propagation
  drops MLE-Bench Lite any-medal from 81.82% to 54.54% — a larger drop than removing the tree structure
  entirely (63.64%). Hierarchy alone isn't enough. What makes it work is that the tree is a substrate
  for accumulating and sharing evidence across branches.
</p>

<div class="callout cyan">
  <p><strong>Executor contract:</strong> Each executor is bound to exactly one hypothesis.
  If the metric stalls, it may repair its implementation — but it cannot change the hypothesis.
  This is what makes backpropagation semantically meaningful: the returned score is evidence
  about <em>that node</em>, not about whatever the executor decided to try instead.</p>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §05 ══ -->
<section id="s-guard" data-code="guard">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Dev/Test Guard</div>
<h2>Separating Exploratory Gains from Verified Progress</h2>
<p>
  Any autonomous system that repeatedly optimizes against a development evaluator will eventually overfit to it.
  Arbor's held-out merge gate is designed to catch exactly this: a candidate is promoted to the current best
  artifact only when it improves over the test set — a held-out split the coordinator <em>cannot</em> use for exploration.
</p>
<p>
  The clearest demonstration is Terminal-Bench. Claude Code achieves the highest development score (75.00)
  but the lowest held-out test score (71.70) — a −3.3 gap that reveals dev-set overfit.
  Arbor has a <em>lower</em> development score (72.22) but the highest test score (77.36), a +5.14 generalization surplus.
</p>

<div class="devtest-grid">
  <div class="devtest-card">
    <div class="devtest-name">Codex (GPT-5.5)</div>
    <div class="devtest-bars" id="dt-codex"></div>
    <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:1rem;font-size:.65rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      <span>Dev</span><span>Test</span>
    </div>
    <div class="dt-delta pos">+3.78 held-out</div>
  </div>
  <div class="devtest-card">
    <div class="devtest-name">Claude Code (Opus 4.6)</div>
    <div class="devtest-bars" id="dt-cc"></div>
    <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:1rem;font-size:.65rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      <span>Dev</span><span>Test</span>
    </div>
    <div class="dt-delta neg">−3.30 overfit gap</div>
  </div>
  <div class="devtest-card arbor">
    <div class="devtest-name">Arbor (Ours)</div>
    <div class="devtest-bars" id="dt-arbor"></div>
    <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:1rem;font-size:.65rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      <span>Dev</span><span>Test</span>
    </div>
    <div class="dt-delta pos">+5.14 generalization</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The dev/test disagreement is informative rather than just noisy: a high-dev, low-test candidate tells the
  coordinator that this direction exploits the feedback signal rather than producing a transferable improvement.
  That information feeds back into the tree — the node gets a specific type of failure label, and its insight
  is tagged as "dev-exploiting" to constrain future hypotheses in that subtree.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════ §06 ══ -->
