    <p>Welcome to this audio summary of Arbor, a framework designed to make autonomous AI research cumulative using Hypothesis Tree Refinement.</p>
    <p>Existing coding and research agents typically treat each experiment as an isolated attempt, failing to retain semantic lessons across trials. Arbor addresses this limitation by organizing research into a structured, growing tree of hypotheses.</p>
    <p>When an agent runs an experiment, it doesn't just log the raw output. Instead, it distills a structured insight, which is then propagated upward through the hypothesis tree. This upward propagation reshapes the parent node's context, ensuring that lessons learned from successes and failures inform all subsequent search branches.</p>
    <p>By preventing the agent from repeating past mistakes and building a cumulative memory of what works, Arbor achieves substantial improvements. Across six complex research tasks, it delivers two point five times the average held-out performance gain of standard developer agents, while producing generalizable research findings rather than overfitting to specific benchmarks.</p>
